  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 916 PM MST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nModels are in good agreement with each other and with satellite\nimagery showing the cut off low descending over southwestern \nArizona into northern Mexico where it will turn to the east \nSaturday to eject into Texas by Monday before turning to the \nnortheast. For eastern Utah and Western Colorado, we'll see a \nridge building east off the Pacific through the weekend bringing \na weak northerly flow aloft keeping skies mostly clear with only\nhigh thin clouds. With all the surface moisture available, \ncan't rule out a light afternoon shower on the higher terrain, \nbut no significant accumulations are likely. With the moist \nground and clear skies, fog may develop in the valleys overnight\ninto the morning hours as about the only weather phenomenon, \nburning off by about 9 AM. This ridge develops into a high in \nthe great Basin by about mid next week, but models are diverging\nby mid week on timing and track of the passing systems. The \ngeneral consensus is another cut off low passing to the south \nalong the Mexican Border about mid week undercutting the ridge, \nand a deep trough and low digging south along the West Coast \nnext weekend to push the high/ridge east out onto the Plains.\nFor the most part, we will see mild, dry weather for the coming\nweek with generally light breezes and daytime temperatures \nrunning about five degrees above normal. It looks like this \nweek will be a good time to finish getting your yard and things \nready for winter, because winter is coming (that's not a \nforecast, just a fact).",
